ial whose roots are \\(a, b, c\\). We form the polynomial:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = (x - a)(x - b)(x - c)\n   \\]\n   Expanding this, we get:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = x^3 - (a+b+c)x^2 + (ab + bc + ca)x - abc\n   \\]\n\n4. Substitute the known values \\(a + b + c\\) and \\(abc\\) into the polynomial:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = x^3 - (a + b + c)x^2 + (ab + bc + ca)x - 1\n   \\]\n   From the given problem, we know:\n   \\[\n   a + b + c = ab + bc + ca\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the polynomial simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = x^3 - (ab + bc + ca)x^2 + (ab + bc + ca)x - 1\n   \\]\n\n5. Let's analyze possible values of \\(a, b, c\\). We set \\(a = 1\\). Then:\n   \\[\n   (a-1)(b-1)(c-1) = 0\n   \\]\n\n6. If \\(a=1\\), then the polynomial becomes:\n   \\[\n   (1-1)(x-b)(x-c) = 0\n   \\]\n   This is trivially true because:\n   \\[\n   0 \\cdot (x-b) \\cdot (x-c) = 0\n   \\]\n\n7. Consequently, if we evaluate using different values of \\(a, b, c\\), the equation \\( (a-1)(b-1)(c-1) = 0 \\) shows that at least one of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), or \\(c\\) must be equal to 1. \n\nTherefore, we have shown that at least one of the numbers \\(a, b\\), or \\(c\\) must be 1.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{One of } a, b, \\text{ or } c \\text{ equals 1.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nVasisualiy Lokhankin has 8 identical cubes. Engineer Ptiburduk took a cube and wrote all the numbers from 1 to 6 on its faces, one number on each face; then he did the same with the remaining cubes. Is it necessarily possible for Vasisualiy to arrange these cubes into a $2 \\times 2 \\times 2$ cube so that the sum of the visible numbers equals 80?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out if Vasisualiy can arrange these 8 cubes into a 2x2x2 cube such that the sum of the visible numbers equals 80. Let me start by understanding the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, there are 8 identical cubes. Each cube has numbers from 1 to 6 on its faces, one