rdinates \\(\\left(0, \\frac{\\lambda d}{1 + \\lambda}\\right)\\).\n     - \\(P\\) has coordinates \\(\\left(\\frac{a + \\lambda c}{1 + \\lambda}, \\frac{\\lambda e}{1 + \\lambda}\\right)\\).\n\n2. **Equation of Line \\(PQ\\):**\n   - The slope of \\(PQ\\) is \\(\\frac{\\lambda(e - d)}{a + \\lambda c}\\).\n   - Equation: \\(y = \\frac{\\lambda(e - d)}{a + \\lambda c}x + \\frac{\\lambda d}{1 + \\lambda}\\).\n\n3. **Intersections \\(E\\) and \\(F\\):**\n   - **Point \\(E\\)** (intersection with \\(AB\\), \\(y = 0\\)):\n     - Solving, \\(x_E = \\frac{-d(a + \\lambda c)}{(1 + \\lambda)(e - d)}\\).\n     - Valid position implies \\(e < d\\).\n   - **Point \\(F\\)** (intersection with \\(CD\\)):\n     - Solving the system of equations for \\(CD\\) and \\(PQ\\) yields coordinates for \\(F\\).\n\n4. **Slopes and Angles:**\n   - **Slope of \\(PE\\):** Calculated as \\(-\\frac{\\lambda(d - e)}{a + \\lambda c}\\).\n   - **Slope of \\(FC\\):** Derived from coordinates, leading to a complex expression.\n\n5. **Trigonometric Relationships:**\n   - \\(\\tan(\\angle BEP)\\) and \\(\\tan(\\angle PFC)\\) are computed using the slopes of \\(PE\\) and \\(FC\\).\n   - Setting \\(\\tan(\\angle BEP) = \\tan(\\angle PFC)\\) and simplifying under the condition \\(\\lambda = \\frac{a}{c}\\) (where \\(AB = a\\) and \\(CD = c\\)) shows the equality holds.\n\n6. **Conclusion:**\n   - When \\(\\lambda = \\frac{AB}{CD}\\), the slopes and hence the angles \\(\\angle BEP\\) and \\(\\angle PFC\\) are equal.\n   - Conversely, if \\(\\angle BEP = \\angle PFC\\), the tangent equality enforces \\(\\lambda = \\frac{AB}{CD}\\).\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\\(\\lambda = \\frac{AB}{CD}\\) if and only if \\(\\angle BEP = \\angle PFC\\), proven through coordinate analysis and trigonometric equality under the given conditions.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Given Details:**\n   - Consider a quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) with points \\(P\\) on the side \\(BC\\) and \\(Q\\) on the side \\(AD\\).\n   - The ratios given