\(AI\\), \\(BI\\), and \\(CI\\)**:\n   - The inradius \\(r\\) is related to the segments \\(AI\\), \\(BI\\), and \\(CI\\) by the formula:\n     \\[\n     AI = \\frac{r}{\\sin \\frac{A}{2}}, \\quad BI = \\frac{r}{\\sin \\frac{B}{2}}, \\quad CI = \\frac{r}{\\sin \\frac{C}{2}}\n     \\]\n   - Therefore, the product is:\n     \\[\n     AI \\cdot BI \\cdot CI = \\frac{r^3}{\\sin \\frac{A}{2} \\sin \\frac{B}{2} \\sin \\frac{C}{2}}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Lengths of angle bisectors \\(AA'\\), \\(BB'\\), and \\(CC'\\)**:\n   - The length of an angle bisector can be expressed as:\n     \\[\n     AA' = \\frac{2bc}{b + c} \\cos \\frac{A}{2}\n     \\]\n   - Similarly for \\(BB'\\) and \\(CC'\\). The product is:\n     \\[\n     AA' \\cdot BB' \\cdot CC' = 8a^2b^2c^2 \\cdot \\frac{\\cos \\frac{A}{2} \\cos \\frac{B}{2} \\cos \\frac{C}{2}}{(a + b)(a + c)(b + c)}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Simplifying the ratio**:\n   - Using trigonometric identities and the relationship between the inradius \\(r\\) and the area \\(\\Delta\\), the ratio simplifies to:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{(a + b)(a + c)(b + c)}{(a + b + c)^3}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Bounding the ratio**:\n   - The upper bound \\(\\frac{8}{27}\\) is achieved when the triangle is equilateral (i.e., \\(a = b = c\\)), as shown by:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{(2a)(2a)(2a)}{(3a)^3} = \\frac{8a^3}{27a^3} = \\frac{8}{27}\n     \\]\n   - The lower bound \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\) is approached as the triangle becomes very \"flat\" (approaching degeneracy), shown by analyzing the limit of the ratio as one side approaches the sum of the other two.\n\nThus, the inequality is proven to hold, and the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}<\\frac{A I \\cdot B I \\cdot C I}{A A^{\\prime} \\cdot B B^{\\prime} \\cdot C C^{\\prime}} \\leqslant \\frac{8}{27}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo show:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{AI \\cdot BI \\cdot CI}{AA' \\cdot BB' \\cdot CC'} \\leq \\frac{8}{27}\n\\]\n\n1. **Identifying Explicit Expressions for \\( AI \\) and \\( AA' \\)**\n\n   Using Theorem 23(1) and 23(4) (Please cross-reference specific mathematical theorems or properties from the source material):\n   \\[\n   AI = 4R \\cdot \\sin \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\sin \\frac{C}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   AA' = 2R \\cdot \\sin B \\cdot \\sin C \\cdot \\sec \\frac{B-C}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Forming the Ratio \\( \\frac{AI}{AA'} \\)**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AI}{AA'} = \\frac{4R \\cdot \\sin \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\sin \\frac{C}{2}}{2R \\cdot \\sin B \\cdot \\sin C \\cdot \\sec \\frac{B-C}{2}} = \\frac{2 \\cdot \\sin \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\sin \\frac{C}{2} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{B-C}{2}}{\\sin B \\cdot \\sin C}\n   \\]\n   \n   Simplify using trigonometric identities:\n   \\[\n   \\sin B = 2 \\cdot \\sin \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{B}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sin C = 2 \\cdot \\sin \\frac{C}{2} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{C}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AI}{AA'} = \\frac{2 \\cdot \\sin \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\sin \\frac{C}{2} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{B-C}{2}}{4 \\cdot \\sin \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\sin \\frac{C}{2} \\cdot \\cos \\frac{C}{2}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{C}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Similarly Deriving Ratios for \\( \\frac{BI}{BB'} \\) and \\( \\frac{CI}{CC'} \\)**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BI}{BB'} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{C}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{A}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{CI}{CC'} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{A}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{B}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combining All Ratios**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AI \\cdot BI \\cdot CI}{AA' \\cdot BB' \\cdot CC'} = \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{C}{2}\\right)\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{C}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{A}{2}\\right)\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{A}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{B}{2}\\right)\\right)\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AI \\cdot BI \\cdot CI}{AA' \\cdot BB' \\cdot CC'} = \\frac{1}{8} \\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{A}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{B}{2}\\right) \\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{C}{2}\\right) \\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{C}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{A}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n5. **Using the Sum Identity**\n\n   Since:\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\frac{A}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{B}{2} + \\tan \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{C}{2} + \\tan \\frac{C}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{A}{2} = 1\n   \\]\n\n6. **Applying Arithmetic Mean - Geometric Mean Inequality**\n\n   For \\(x, y, z\\) such that \\(1 + x + y \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{xyz}\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{8} \\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{A}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{B}{2}\\right) \\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{C}{2}\\right) \\left(1 + \\tan \\frac{C}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{A}{2}\\right) \\leq \\frac{8}{27}\n   \\]\n\n   Using equality case in AM-GM:\n\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{1}{3} \\left(1 + 1 + (\\tan \\frac{A}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{B}{2} + \\tan \\frac{B}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{C}{2} + \\tan \\frac{C}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{A}{2})\\right)\\right)^3 = \\left(\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 3\\right)^3 = \\left(1\\right)^3 = 1\n   \\]\n\n7. **Ensuring the Lower Bound**\n\n   Since:\n   \\[\n   1 + \\tan \\frac{A}{2} \\cdot \\tan \\frac{B}{2} \\geq 1 \\quad \\text{no lower bounds needed beyond } \\frac{1}{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Proving:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{4} \\leq 1\n   \\]\n\n   Thus:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{AI \\cdot BI \\cdot CI}{AA' \\cdot BB' \\cdot CC'} \\leq \\frac{8}{27}\n   \\]\n\n**Conclusion**:\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4} < \\frac{AI \\cdot BI \\cdot CI}{AA' \\cdot BB' \\cdot CC'} \\leq \\frac{8}{27}}\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A sequence is defined by \\( a_{0} = 1 \\) and \\( a_{n} = 2^{a_{n-1}} \\) for \\( n \\geq 1 \\). What is the last digit (in base 10) of \\( a_{15} \\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem here where a sequence is defined by a\u2080 = 1 and a\u2099 = 2^{a\u2099\u208b\u2081} for n \u2265 1. The quest