ect (3,-1) back to (0,1), that last side would cross over previous sides. So, it's not a simple polygon.\n\nAh, there's the problem. To have a simple polygon where each side crosses the line, it must not intersect itself. So, how to arrange the vertices so that the polygon is simple, non-convex, and all sides are crossed by a single line.\n\nAlternatively, think of a spiral-shaped polygon. But a spiral is not a closed polygon. Hmm. Maybe a polygon that winds around a central point multiple times, but again, that would likely be self-intersecting.\n\nWait, perhaps another approach. If the polygon is constructed such that it has multiple \"layers\" around a line, each layer contributing two sides that cross the line. For instance, imagine a horizontal line, and the polygon has several humps above and below the line. Each hump contributes two sides that cross the line. If there are n humps, that's 2n sides. By making the polygon weave above and below the line n times, each time crossing the line with two sides, then a horizontal line would intersect all 2n sides. But again, ensuring that the polygon is simple (non-intersecting) might be challenging.\n\nLet me try constructing such a polygon with 4 sides (n=2). Start at the left below the line, go up crossing the line to a peak, then down crossing the line to a valley, then up crossing the line again to another peak, then down back to the starting point. Wait, but that would create a polygon with four sides, each crossing the line. However, connecting back to the starting point would require the last side to cross the line as well. But in this case, starting at (0,0), go to (1,1) [crossing up], then to (2,-1) [crossing down], then to (3,1) [crossing up], then back to (0,0) [crossing down]. But the last segment from (3,1) to (0,0) is a diagonal that crosses the line y=0 at some point. Therefore, the line y=0 intersects all four sides. However, the polygon is self-intersecting because the segment from (3,1) to (0,0) would