ge this:\n\n(E1 + E2 + E3) > (E8 + E9 + E10) - E4.\n\nBut that doesn't directly help. Alternatively, maybe we can bound E8 in terms of E4.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can use the ordering of the elephants.\n\nSince E1 \u2264 E2 \u2264 ... \u2264 E10, we know that E4 \u2264 E5 \u2264 E6 \u2264 E7 \u2264 E8 \u2264 E9 \u2264 E10.\n\nTherefore, E8 \u2265 E7 \u2265 E6 \u2265 E5 \u2265 E4.\n\nSo, substituting into the original inequality:\n\nE1 + E2 + E3 + E4 > E8 + E9 + E10 \u2265 E4 + E9 + E10.\n\nWait, since E8 \u2265 E4, right? Because E4 is the fourth elephant and E8 is the eighth, so E8 \u2265 E4.\n\nTherefore, substituting:\n\nE1 + E2 + E3 + E4 > E8 + E9 + E10 \u2265 E4 + E9 + E10.\n\nSubtracting E4 from both sides:\n\nE1 + E2 + E3 > E9 + E10.\n\nAh! So if we take the original inequality E1 + E2 + E3 + E4 > E8 + E9 + E10, and since E8 \u2265 E4, then replacing E8 with E4 gives a weaker inequality:\n\nE1 + E2 + E3 + E4 > E4 + E9 + E10.\n\nSubtract E4 from both sides:\n\nE1 + E2 + E3 > E9 + E10.\n\nSo that's a key result. Therefore, the sum of the three lightest elephants is greater than the sum of the two heaviest elephants.\n\nTherefore, if we take any three elephants on the left, even the lightest three (E1, E2, E3), their total weight is greater than the heaviest two (E9, E10). Therefore, when comparing three elephants on the left versus two on the right, even the lightest three on the left will outweigh the heaviest two on the right. Hence, any three elephants on the left will outweigh any two on the right.\n\nTherefore, the left pan will necessarily tip over.\n\nWait, but let me double-check this logic. The original condition gives that the four lightest are heavier than the three heaviest. Then, by substituting E8 with E4 (since E8 \u2265 E4), we get that E1 + E2 + E3 + E4 > E4 + E9 + E10, leading to E1 + E2 + E3 > E9 + E10. Therefore, the three lightest are heavier than the two heaviest. Therefore, even if you take the three lightest on the left, they will o