ould be heavier than E1-E3), their total would be higher than 30, while the right pan would have two elephants, say E1 and E2, total 20. So left is even heavier.\n\nAlternatively, if left has E1, E2, E5 and right has E9, E10, then left is 10 + 10 + (some value \u226510). Let's say E5 is 11. So left is 31, right is 27. Still left is heavier.\n\nHence, in this constructed example, the three vs. two case always results in left being heavier.\n\nAlternatively, let's try another example where the four lightest just barely outweigh the three heaviest.\n\nSuppose E1=1, E2=1, E3=1, E4=1. Total four lightest: 4.\n\nThen E8 + E9 + E10 must be less than 4. But since elephants are ordered, E8 \u2265 E4=1. So E8, E9, E10 are each at least 1. So the minimal total for three heaviest is 1+1+1=3, but the four lightest total 4, so 4 > 3. Then, the three lightest (1+1+1=3) vs. two heaviest (1+1=2). Then left would be 3 vs. right 2. So left is heavier. But in this case, all elephants weigh the same. Wait, but if all elephants have equal weight, then four on the left would weigh 4 units, three on the right 3 units. So left is heavier. Then three on the left would be 3 vs. two on the right 2, left still heavier. So in this case, yes.\n\nBut what if there's a different distribution?\n\nWait, maybe the elephants' weights aren't all the same. Suppose that the four lightest are just barely enough to outweigh the three heaviest.\n\nLet me try:\n\nE1=1, E2=1, E3=1, E4=2. Total four lightest: 1+1+1+2=5.\n\nThen the three heaviest (E8, E9, E10) must be less than 5. Let's say E8=1, E9=1, E10=2. Their total: 1+1+2=4 <5. So the given condition is satisfied.\n\nNow, check three vs. two. The three lightest (1,1,1) sum to 3. The two heaviest (1,2) sum to 3. So left pan (3) equals right pan (3). So in this case, the pans balance. But the problem says \"if any four elephants stand on the left... the left pan will tip over.\" So in this constructed example, the condition is barely satisfied (4 left vs 3 