 -4)/7 +1=2009/7 +1=287 +1=288. Correct.\n\nTherefore, the total is indeed 288 +289 +289 +288=1154.\n\nBut let me cross-validate.\n\nAlternatively, note that in the range1 to N, where N=2019, the number of numbers congruent to a mod7 is floor((N -a)/7) +1.\n\nFor a=0: floor(2019/7)=288, since 7*288=2016, so 2016 is the last multiple of7 less than2019. Hence, numbers:7,14,...,2016:288 numbers.\n\nFor a=1: floor((2019 -1)/7) +1= floor(2018/7)=288 (since288*7=2016, 2018-1=2017, 2017/7\u2248288.14). Wait, but formula is floor((N -a)/7) +1. So for a=1:\n\nfloor((2019 -1)/7)=floor(2018/7)=288 (2018=7*288 + 2). Then +1 gives289.\n\nSimilarly, for a=3:\n\nfloor((2019 -3)/7)=floor(2016/7)=288, +1=289.\n\nFor a=4:\n\nfloor((2019 -4)/7)=floor(2015/7)=287 (2015=7*287 +6), so floor(2015/7)=287, +1=288. So same as before. So total is288+289+289+288=1154.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be1154.\n\nWait, but I need to make sure that there are no overlaps between these residue classes. Since 0,1,3,4 mod7 are distinct residues. 0,1,3,4 are all different modulo7, so there is no overlap. Therefore, the counts can be safely added.\n\nTherefore, the number of elements in A\u2229B is1154.\n\nBut let me check with smaller numbers to ensure my reasoning is correct.\n\nSuppose B was {1,2,...,14}. Then A would consist of numbers:\n\nFor n=0:1,3,4\n\nn=1:7,8,10,11\n\nn=2:14,15,17,18 but 15,17,18 are beyond14. So in B={1,...,14}, the numbers would be1,3,4,7,8,10,11,14.\n\nTotal:8 numbers.\n\nAccording to the formula:\n\nResidues0,1,3,4 mod7.\n\nIn 1-14:\n\n0 mod7:7,14\u21922 numbers\n\n1 mod7:1,8\u21922 numbers\n\n3 mod7:3,10\u21922 numbers\n\n4 mod7:4,11\u21922 numbers\n\nTotal:2+2+2+2=8. Correct.\n\nSo the formula works here.\n\nAnother test case with B={1,...,7}:\n\nA\u2229B would be1,3,4,7. Total4 numbers.\n\nResidues:\n\n0 mod7:7\u21921\n\n1 mod7:1\u21921\n\n3 mod7:3\u21921\n\n4 mod7:4\u21921\n\nTotal4. Correct.\n\nAnother test with B={1,...,10}:\n\nNumbers from A:1,3,4,7,8,10. Total6.\n\n