     \\]\n     Substituting \\(x = 4\\) into \\(y = -x + 8\\) gives \\(y = 4\\). Thus, \\(E(4, 4)\\).\n\n3. **Using the shoelace formula to find the area of pentagon \\(ABCDE\\)**:\n   - Coordinates of the vertices in order: \\(A(0, 2)\\), \\(B(1, 7)\\), \\(C(10, 7)\\), \\(D(7, 1)\\), \\(E(4, 4)\\), and back to \\(A(0, 2)\\).\n   - Applying the shoelace formula:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (x_i y_{i+1} - x_{i+1} y_i) \\right|\n     \\]\n     Calculations:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{aligned}\n     &0 \\cdot 7 - 1 \\cdot 2 = -2, \\\\\n     &1 \\cdot 7 - 10 \\cdot 7 = -63, \\\\\n     &10 \\cdot 1 - 7 \\cdot 7 = -39, \\\\\n     &7 \\cdot 4 - 4 \\cdot 1 = 24, \\\\\n     &4 \\cdot 2 - 0 \\cdot 4 = 8.\n     \\end{aligned}\n     \\]\n     Summing these values:\n     \\[\n     -2 - 63 - 39 + 24 + 8 = -72\n     \\]\n     Taking the absolute value and multiplying by \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| -72 \\right| = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 72 = 36\n     \\]\n\n4. **Verification by dividing into triangles**:\n   - Calculating the areas of triangles \\(ABE\\), \\(BCE\\), and \\(CDE\\) using the formula for the area of a triangle with coordinates:\n     - Area of \\(\\triangle ABE = 9\\)\n     - Area of \\(\\triangle BCE = 13.5\\)\n     - Area of \\(\\triangle CDE = 13.5\\)\n   - Summing these areas: \\(9 + 13.5 + 13.5 = 36\\).\n\nThus, the area of pentagon \\(ABCDE\\) is \\(\\boxed{36}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Equation of the line through points \\( A \\) and \\( C \\)**:\n    \n    Given points \\( A(0, 2) \\) and \\( C(10, 7) \\), the equation of the line passing through these points is:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{y-y_1}{y_2-y_1} = \\frac{x-x_1}{x_2-x_1}\n    \\]\n    Substituting \\( (x_1, y_1) = (0, 2) \\) and \\( (x_2, y_2) = (10, 7) \\):\n    \\[\n    \\frac{y-2}{7-2} = \\frac{x-0}{10-0}\n    \\implies \\frac{y-2}{5} = \\frac{x}{10}\n    \\implies y - 2 = \\frac{1}{2}x\n    \\implies y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 2\n    \\]\n\n2. **Equation of the line through points \\( B \\) and \\( D \\)**:\n\n    Given points \\( B(1, 7) \\) and \\( D(7, 1) \\), the equation of the line passing through these points is:\n    \\[\n    \\frac{y-y_1}{y_2-y_1} = \\frac{x-x_1}{x_2-x_1}\n    \\]\n    Substituting \\( (x_1, y_1) = (1, 7) \\) and \\( (x_2, y_2) = (7, 1) \\):\n    \\[\n    \\frac{y-7}{1-7} = \\frac{x-1}{6}\n    \\implies \\frac{y-7}{-6} = \\frac{x-1}{6}\n    \\implies y - 7 = -x + 1\n    \\implies y = -x + 8\n    \\]\n\n3. **Finding the intersection point \\( E \\)**:\n\n    To find the coordinates of point \\( E \\), we need to solve the system:\n    \\[\n    \\begin{cases}\n    y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 2 \\\\\n    y = -x + 8\n    \\end{cases}\n    \\]\n    Substitute \\( y \\) from the second equation into the first:\n    \\[\n    -x + 8 = \\frac{1}{2}x + 2\n    \\implies 8 - 2 = \\frac{1}{2}x + x\n    \\implies 6 = \\frac{3}{2}x\n    \\implies x = 4\n    \\]\n    Substituting \\( x = 4 \\) back into \\( y = -x + 8 \\):\n    \\[\n    y = -4 + 8 = 4\n    \\]\n    Thus, the coordinates of \\( E \\) are \\( (4, 4) \\).\n\n4. **Area of the pentagon \\( \\text{ABCDE} \\)**:\n\n    Using the trapezoidal decomposition method for the three triangles:\n    \\[\n    S_{\\text{ABCDE}} = S_{\\Delta BAC} + S_{\\Delta BDC} - S_{\\Delta BEC}\n    \\]\n    \n    - Area of \\( \\Delta BAC \\):\n        \\[\n        BC = |x_C - x_B| = 10 - 1 = 9\n        \\]\n        Height \\( h_A = 7 - 2 = 5 \\)\n        \\[\n        S_{\\Delta BAC} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times BC \\times h_A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 9 \\times 5 = 22.5\n        \\]\n\n    - Area of \\( \\Delta BDC \\):\n        \\[\n        h_D = 7 - 1 = 6\n        \\]\n        \\[\n        S_{\\Delta BDC} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 9 \\times 6 = 27\n        \\]\n\n    - Area of \\( \\Delta BEC \\):\n        \\[\n        h_E = 7 - 4 = 3\n        \\]\n        \\[\n        S_{\\Delta BEC} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 9 \\times 3 = 13.5\n        \\]\n\n    There