hen the perpendicular distance from M to AB is |y_M - b|. But since AB is a side of the rectangle, and we are dropping a perpendicular from M to AB, point P is the foot of this perpendicular. Similarly, MQ is the foot of the perpendicular from M to CD, which is y = -b.\n\nWait, but if M is on the circumcircle, which has radius sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2), then the coordinates of M can be (sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) cos \u03b8, sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) sin \u03b8). Let me check if that's correct. For example, point A is (-a, b), so (-a)^2 + b^2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2, which is correct. Similarly for other vertices. So M is (sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) cos \u03b8, sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) sin \u03b8). \n\nSo, the foot of the perpendicular from M to AB (y = b) would be the point P with the same x-coordinate as M and y-coordinate b. But wait, if M is above AB, then the foot would be on AB, but if M is below AB, the foot would still be on AB? Wait, no. If we drop a perpendicular from M to AB, regardless of where M is, the foot P is the closest point on AB to M. Since AB is horizontal, the perpendicular is vertical. So, if M has coordinates (x, y), then P is (x, b) if y \u2265 b, or (x, b) if y \u2264 b? Wait, no. If M is above AB (y > b), then the foot would be (x, b). If M is below AB (y < b), the perpendicular would still be (x, b), but that point is still on AB. Wait, AB is the line segment from (-a, b) to (a, b). So, if M is outside the rectangle, the foot of the perpendicular might not lie on the side AB, but on its extension. Wait, but the problem says \"perpendiculars MP and MQ were dropped to two of its opposite sides\". So even if M is outside the rectangle, we still drop the perpendicular to the side, not the extension. Wait, but the problem also mentions \"perpendiculars MR and MT were dropped to the extensions of the other two sides\". So, for two opposite sides, we drop perpendiculars directly to the sides (even if M is outside, the foot is on the side), and for the other two sides, we drop to their extensions.\n\nWait, but how can we drop a perpendicular from M to a side if M is on the circumcircle? The circumcircle passes through all four vertices. So, depending on where M is, it can be inside or outside the rectangle. Wait, no. The rectangle is on its circumcircle, but actually, all four vertices lie on the circumcircle. The rectangle itself is inside the circle. Wait, the rectangle is inscribed in the circle. So, the circle has a radius equal to half the diagonal of the rectangle. So, any point on the circle is at a distance equal to half the diagonal from the center. The rectangle's sides are shorter than the diameter of the circle. So, points on the circle can be outside the rectangle. For example, if you take a point on the circumcircle that's not one of the vertices, it's likely outside the rectangle.\n\nTherefore, dropping a perpendicular from M to a side might result in the foot being on the extension of the side. But the problem specifies that MP and MQ are dropped to two opposite sides, meaning the feet P and Q are on the sides themselves, not their extensions. Whereas MR and MT are dropped to the extensions of the other two sides. So, for sides AB and CD (horizontal sides), we drop perpendiculars to the sides (so P and Q are on AB and CD respectively, even if M is outside the rectangle). For the vertical sides AD and BC, we drop perpendiculars to their extensions. So, if AD is from (-a, b) to (-a, -b), its extension would be beyond -b or beyond b. Similarly for BC.\n\nTherefore, in coordinates, if we have M (sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) cos \u03b8, sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) sin \u03b8), then:\n\n- MP is the foot of the perpendicular from M to AB (y = b). Since AB is the line segment from (-a, b) to (a, b), the foot P would have coordinates (x_M, b). However, if x_M is outside [-a, a], then the foot would actually be on the extension of AB, but the problem states that MP is dropped to the side AB. Wait, this is confusing. If M is